    "created_at": "2026-01-29T15:27:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-29T15:27:48Z",
    "body": "Agreed that having an explicit common global cleanup step affordance would be great.  I think [web-locks attempting to specify its behavior when a global terminates](https://w3c.github.io/web-locks/#termination-of-locks) is a great example of something that would benefit.  This is especially true for workers \n\nIn Gecko we have a mix-in mechanism called the [GlobalTeardownObserver](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/source/dom/base/GlobalTeardownObserver.h) that registers with our common [nsIGlobalObject](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/source/dom/base/nsIGlobalObject.h) superclass shared by all globals which then has an explicit point in the global lifecycles where we tell the GTO's to disconnect ([diagram](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/query/default?q=calls-to%3A%27nsIGlobalObject%3A%3ADisconnectGlobalTeardownObservers%27+depth%3A2)).\n\nGecko globals have a concept of the global [beginning the process of shutting down (\"dying\")](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/rev/0fc599421fc27ac61c501f277cb440777f85b18a/dom/base/nsIGlobalObject.h#93-107) ([initiating call diagram](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/query/default?q=calls-to%3A%27nsIGlobalObject%3A%3AStartDying%27+depth%3A2)) and our worker impl is notable in that we mark the global as starting to shutdown when we interrupt anything running on the worker as a result of terminate() or similar, but we don't disconnect the GTO's until we have unwound back to the bare event loop.  I believe this is consistent with the obvious place to put a cleanup hook where we currently clear the timers/etc.\n\n",
